  "before_interfaces": {
    "lem-tgrx-finite-matrix-entropy-variation": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nLet $H$ be a nonzero finite-dimensional complex inner-product space with a fixed orthonormal basis, let $J\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open interval, and let $t_0\\in J$. Suppose $A:J\\to\\operatorname{End}(H)$ has real and imaginary matrix entries in the fixed basis differentiable at $t_0$ and is self-adjoint with strictly positive eigenvalues on a neighborhood of $t_0$. Define $\\log A(t)$ by applying the natural logarithm to the eigenvalues of $A(t)$ in its spectral decomposition, let $\\operatorname{tr}$ be the finite-dimensional trace, and let $\\|T\\|_{\\mathrm{op}}:=\\sup_{\\|v\\|=1}\\|Tv\\|$ be the operator norm. Then $G(t):=\\operatorname{tr}(A(t)\\log A(t))$ is differentiable at $t_0$ and\n\n$$G'(t_0)=\\operatorname{tr}\\!\\left(A'(t_0)(\\log A(t_0)+I_H)\\right),$$\n\nwhere $I_H$ is the identity endomorphism of $H$ and $A'(t_0)$ is obtained by differentiating the matrix entries in the fixed basis."
    ],
    "prop-tgrx-finite-modular-balance": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nLet $H$ be a nonzero finite-dimensional complex inner-product space. Let $\\rho,\\sigma:H\\to H$ be self-adjoint linear maps with nonnegative eigenvalues and trace one, and suppose every eigenvalue of $\\sigma$ is strictly positive. Define $K_\\sigma:=-\\log\\sigma$ by spectral calculus, and for any self-adjoint $\\tau:H\\to H$ with nonnegative eigenvalues and trace one define $s(\\tau):=-\\operatorname{tr}(\\tau\\log\\tau)=-\\sum_{\\lambda_j(\\tau)>0}\\lambda_j(\\tau)\\log\\lambda_j(\\tau)$, with the zero-eigenvalue term $0\\log0:=0$ and natural logarithms, so the entropy is in nats. Define the modular-energy difference relative to $\\sigma$ by $\\Delta_K(\\rho,\\sigma):=\\operatorname{tr}((\\rho-\\sigma)K_\\sigma)$. Then\n\n$$D(\\rho\\|\\sigma)=\\Delta_K(\\rho,\\sigma)-(s(\\rho)-s(\\sigma)).$$\n\nFor any nonzero finite-dimensional complex inner-product spaces $H_A,H_B$ with $H=H_A\\otimes H_B$, partial tracing both states over $H_B$ cannot increase relative entropy:\n\n$$D(\\operatorname{Tr}_{B}\\rho\\|\\operatorname{Tr}_{B}\\sigma)\\le D(\\rho\\|\\sigma).$$\n\nThe identity and inequality are finite-dimensional mathematical statements; no Hamiltonian, physical energy, physical units, continuum limit, or gravitational entropy is assumed."
    ],
    "prop-tgrx-entropy-first-variation": [
      null,
      "## Statement\n\nLet $H$ be a nonzero finite-dimensional complex inner-product space and let $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ be an open interval containing $0$. Let $\\rho:I\\to\\operatorname{End}(H)$ be a path whose real and imaginary matrix entries are twice continuously differentiable, such that each $\\rho(\\varepsilon)$ is self-adjoint with nonnegative eigenvalues and trace one. Suppose $\\rho(0)=\\sigma$ and every eigenvalue of $\\sigma$ is strictly positive. Define $K_\\sigma:=-\\log\\sigma$ by spectral calculus and let $s(\\tau):=-\\operatorname{tr}(\\tau\\log\\tau)$ with the finite spectral convention $0\\log0:=0$. Then\n\n$$\\left.\\frac{d}{d\\varepsilon}s(\\rho(\\varepsilon))\\right|_{\\varepsilon=0}=\\operatorname{tr}(\\rho'(0)K_\\sigma).$$\n\nThe finite relative entropy $D(\\rho(\\varepsilon)\\|\\sigma)$ has zero first derivative at $\\varepsilon=0$. These are finite-dimensional mathematical statements and make no continuum or gravitational claim."
    ],
    "ex-tgrx-qubit-first-variation": [
      null
    ]
  }
